From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 23:09:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: the shared-expert down projection co-launched
 inside the routed expert-reduce (+2.6% decode)

Round 34 measured t(d) = 4448 us + 2.99 us/dispatch and priced a removed
MATVEC launch at 2.99 + ~2.85 = 5.8 us, because it takes a bandwidth ramp with
it. The shared-expert down projection is 40 such launches per decoded token: a
plain Q8_0 matvec whose output width is exactly the routed expert-reduce's
(n_embd), over a completely independent activation.

Merging the two needs the one piece of machinery the series did not have - a
SECOND vec_dot type in one launch - and it is bit-identical by construction,
exactly as the 0045 F32 tail is: the guest blocks run the
mul_mat_vec_q<Q8_0, 1, ...> body verbatim, with the k-block assignment derived
from the guest type's own compile-time nwarps, the cross-warp partial exchange
including the identically-+0.0f slots the host warp trim leaves behind, the
same warp_reduce_sum and the same 0035 dst-scale epilogue. Warps-per-row is
whatever the standalone launch would use (2 here), so blockDim.y/2 = 4 rows
share a block and the merged grid is 512 guest + 2048 host blocks with exactly
the wave count the two separate launches had.

The graph half is the harder half, and it is an ALLOCATOR problem rather than a
detector problem (round 31's lesson). Stock order runs the shared-expert tail
after the routed reduction, where ggml-alloc has already freed the expert ids
and routing weights and hands the shared-expert output their block - which the
merged launch would still be reading. build_moe_ffn now relays a caller-supplied
tail between the routed activation and the routed down projection, so the whole
shared-expert tail (sigmoid gate, swiglu, down projection, gate multiply) is
emitted there: the two matvecs become adjacent AND the shared-expert output is
allocated while the ids and weights are still live, which is what lets the
cross-product disjointness check pass at all. Only node order moves - same ops,
same operands, same kernels - and it is gated on n_tokens == 1, so prefill is
the stock build path.

Census: 681.1 -> 641.1 dispatches/token, exactly -40.0, mul_mat_vec_q 4585 ->
3185 per 35 tokens and nothing else moved. tg128 157.31 vs 153.31 against a
binary built from this patch's parent commit (control RUNPATH verified by ldd,
arms alternated, no env set on either arm), 4/4 separated, +2.61%; pp512 flat.
Decode-path perplexity (-b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8, runner corpus) is
BIT-IDENTICAL: 3.9382 on both arms across three loads each.

GGML_CUDA_SHEXP_DOWN_COLAUNCH=0 restores the two separate launches;
GGML_QWEN_SHEXP_TAIL_PIN=0 restores stock node order.
